"note": "English-origin candidate, unconfirmed: a baptism on 20 Feb 1740 at St Denis, York (England, Select Births and Christenings, 1538-1975 — “John Whitton”, son of John Whitton) matches his birth year; no linking record yet ties it to this John. A 1764 Northamptonshire marriage of a same-named John Whitton (bride Elizabeth Ridgeway) was checked and belongs to a different family (rejected). The deepest generation on the Collins side so far. Candidate: an 1815 Clarke Co., Georgia property tax digest lists a \"John Whitton\" (Capt. Smith's District) — the county where the family lived by 1820 — but this John W. is recorded as dying in 1810, so it may be a younger same-name man. A 1748 Northamptonshire poll book (Marston St Laurence) is the same English family as the rejected 1764 marriage, not this John. REJECTED 2026-10-15: the Northamptonshire, North Yorkshire and London records attached to this card are English namesakes (Tiffield, Northamptonshire — the 1764 John Whitton who married Elizabeth Ridgeway — plus the Helmdon Whitton family and a 1743 London land-tax “John Whiton”); not used. ROUND 53 (2026-10-16): the 17 Jun 1795 Fauquier marriage record names the bride as Alice Ore (Virginia Select Marriages, film 31633) — the card previously carried this marriage without a bride; the family's Elizabeth Hinson reading for it is unconfirmed, both kept. Candidate: a “John Whitton,” 24, arriving in Maryland in 1764 (Passenger and Immigration Lists Index, from “Maryland Runaway Convict Servants, 1745-1780”) — the age matches a 1740 birth; no linking record yet. A John Whitton appears in the Revolutionary War Rolls (14th Regiment, Virginia) and a “John Whitton” is in the 1783 Virginia census substitute at Amherst County — candidates for this John, unconfirmed. The AGBI entry reads birth 1740-1749, Virginia, citing “Rev.war Recd.va:825.”\n\nROUND 60 (2026-10-16, potentials sweep): a naturalization lead was read — \"Denizations and Naturalizations in the British Colonies in America, 1607-1775\" (p. 322) reads: \"Whitton, John. He was naturalized in Augusta County, Virginia 18 May 1774.\" Recorded as a candidate: the volume names no parents or origin, so it stays an unlinked possible identification with this John (it would fit an immigrant birth, consistent with the unproven English reading). The \"Bonded Passengers to America\" entries for the name were checked and set aside — they are earlier men (John Whitton transported 1731 to Maryland; Matthew Whitten/Witton, 1719, ship Worcester to Maryland) and this John's recorded birth is 1740.",
